{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"agent_message","text":"**SIGN-OFF WITHHELD.** The implementation review found no clear violation of the L1 runtime design, but three explicit acceptance claims still lack direct assertions. The author’s reported JVM, Node, and Dart suites pass; I did not rerun them in this read-only review.\n\n| Severity | file:line | Issue | Fix |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Medium | [dht_test.cljc:760](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-linker-l1/test/dao/space/dht_test.cljc:760) | The sustained dead-network check bounds queue and publication counts, but does not check the required **ledger-entry bound at every step**. The separate overflow test checks it only at selected points. | Add `:ledger-entries ≤ (:max-open + :max-repairing) × ring-capacity` to the per-step check, including the shared-address run. |\n| Medium | [dht_test.cljc:977](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-linker-l1/test/dao/space/dht_test.cljc:977) | “No repair cycle ever opens” for the shared `/sent` case is inferred from one request and retirement. A cycle could open without issuing a second request. | Assert `:cycles` remains zero on each step until that publication retires. |\n| Low | [dht_test.cljc:772](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-linker-l1/test/dao/space/dht_test.cljc:772) | “No put ever waits” is left as a construction argument rather than an acceptance assertion. | In the dead-network test, complete puts and announcements before stepping the node or peers, and assert that their local verdicts are returned. |\n\nThe **one chosen non-manifest blob** case is directly covered by the plain Clojure mesh test. The REPL’s broader refusal of non-manifest chunk stores is acceptable for L1 because those frames do not carry an address. The shared-address overflow fix, fixed ledgers, separate queue capacities, repair-first admission, pacing through `/replicated` or `/unacknowledged`, cancellation, result-change events, and staged-load outcomes are consistent with the contract on inspection. Swapping the request writer is acceptable white-box access for the otherwise hard-to-produce `:busy` and `:unaskable` outcomes.\n\nThe added read functions and the `retry!`, `cancel!`, and `forget` refusal codes are useful public API. **§10 and §9 should name their stable shapes and codes** so callers need not infer them from tests. I found no added `yin.*` dependency in `dao.space.dht`, no `dao.jing` change, or new node role. The reported three-host runs provide portability evidence, subject to the assertion gaps above."}}
